  - [38;5;153mhandlers.js:36[39m — [38;5;153mcreatedAt[39m and [38;5;153mtotal[39m come from the client body unvalidated. Only [38;5;153mid[39m is checked. The repo already
    ships [38;5;153mnowIso(clock)[39m in [38;5;153mutil.js:3[39m for server-side timestamping, injectable for tests, and the new code bypasses it.
  - [38;5;153mhandlers.js:24[39m — the response has no [38;5;153mtotal[39m/[38;5;153mhasMore[39m, so a client can't tell the last page from an out-of-range one.
  - [38;5;153mconfig.js:3-9[39m — config moved to a runtime [38;5;153mJSON.parse(readFileSync(...))[39m. I confirmed the values are byte-identical
    to the literals they replaced, so it buys no configurability, and it adds a startup crash path with no error
    context. This is a third change neither part of the commit message mentions.

  [1mMinor:[0m [38;5;153mwithRetry[39m wraps [38;5;153mstore.listOrders[39m (an [38;5;153mArray.slice[39m that cannot fail) while [38;5;153msaveOrder[39m, the one that does throw,
  gets no retry; [38;5;153mwithRetry[39m throws a bare [38;5;153mundefined[39m if [38;5;153mattempts < 1[39m; the two exported handlers now have different calling
  conventions; and [38;5;153mlistOrdersHandler[39m went sync-returning-all-orders to async-returning-a-page while [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m still
  declares it [38;5;153mmain[39m at [38;5;153m0.1.0[39m — I confirmed the old signature was [38;5;153mlistOrdersHandler()[39m with no arguments, so any existing
  caller silently reads [38;5;153m.orders[39m off a Promise.

  [1mMy read on the findings[0m

  The two Criticals and the test-gap finding are solid and I reproduced the reasoning. The config-externalization item
  (#6) is the weakest of the "Important" set — it's largely a scope-and-taste argument, though the new import-time crash
  path is a genuine regression. Treat it as optional-or-split rather than blocking.

